---
title: The Bell Jar
subject: Sylvia Plath
genre: Books
rating: 4
cover: /posters/the-bell-jar.jpg
date: 2026-09-01
excerpt: >-
  The bell jar never went away, it merely moved shape along with Esther.
---

The final image of The Bell Jar is not one of freedom, it's of falsehood. The bell jar never went away, it merely moved shape along with Esther. Sylvia Plath's The Bell Jar explores the descent of a woman's mind who is in the public eye and carries the expectations of society. It answers the question: what happens to a woman who is expected to give much to society with nothing in return? Plath represents Esther as a manifestation of her own dark path as her life turned and flipped on its head. The bell jar first appeared when she lost her father, when the only person that let her be free without judgement was taken from her, trapping her in this glass jar for society to scrutinize and perfect.

![The William Heinemann edition, still credited to the pseudonym Victoria Lucas](/posters/the-bell-jar-1.jpg)

Enter Buddy, the first half of her story. Buddy represents the idealistic side of her mind. She yearns for love, for loyalty, to somehow fill that void that had been missing for years. Buddy represents a falsehood of this. He gives her letters, he says sweet things, but ultimately he is sleeping with other women on the side. There is a brilliant moment at the end of her ideal version of him where she says "if you expect nothing from somebody, you are never disappointed." This is the second descent for Esther. In the second half of the book we explore her mind and relationships while in an asylum, specifically following Joan, who is the foil to Esther. Plath sees herself and Esther both in Joan, a woman so nearly free, who doesn't make it. She is just as smart and intelligent as Esther but from a richer and more privileged background. When she ultimately hangs herself it is Esther's final descent into complete numbness and coldness. When Joan breaks, so does Esther's last belief that escape was possible. All Esther wants is to feel loved and cherished by the people around her and at every turn she is failed. Once by her dying father, next by her betraying boyfriend, next by a last ditch attempt to find love in someone she never could truly fall for, feeling the most pain after a night with him, and lastly by the one girl she saw as a mirror of herself.

![The Faber and Faber 50th anniversary edition, 2013](/posters/the-bell-jar-2.jpg)

Ultimately the book ends in emphatic and profound fashion. It seems as though Esther has found freedom and escaped the bell jar she was trapped in throughout the story, but Plath turns this on its head. She finishes with Esther entering a room to a set of eyes watching and judging her every move, showing that she never truly escaped at all, unable to reach happiness because the eyes of society are still on her. The bell jar was always there from the beginning. It just took a new shape.
